    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d121">

                <pb facs="0173" n="83" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg83"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1361</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vologda</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-03-21"><hi rendition="#italic">March 21, 1918</hi></date>.
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received March 25, 9.35 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>25. Repeated message from Macgowan<anchor n="1" xml:id="d121-f1"/> hitherto
                    undecipherable confirms Moran’s<anchor n="2" xml:id="d121-f2"/> report about
                    prisoners at Irkutsk. Summers says Germans advancing from south without
                    resistance. Morris cables Emery<anchor n="3" xml:id="d121-f3"/> taken, sent
                    Berlin. Crosley, Stines and others at Björneborg.</p>
                <pb facs="0174" n="84" xml:id="pg84"/>
                <p>Trotsky denies prisoners being armed and Robins<anchor n="1" xml:id="d121-f4"/>
                    credits denial saying Russian officer and Webster<anchor n="2" xml:id="d121-f5"
                    /> with British officer who <hi rendition="#italic">en route</hi> Irkutsk to
                    investigate going only to convince me that reports of prisoners organizing are
                    untrue.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Francis</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d121-f1" n="1" xml:id="d121-f1-content">
                <p>David B. Macgowan, Consul at Moscow, on detail in Siberia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d121-f2" n="2" xml:id="d121-f2-content">
                <p>Hugh Moran, representative of the Y.M.C.A. in Siberia; see <ref
                        target="frus1918Russiav01#pg_483">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>,
                        p. 483</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d121-f3" n="3" xml:id="d121-f3-content">
                <p>Henry C. Emery, in 1917 American member of the Inter-Ally Priority Board at
                    Petrograd (Michelsen commission), was arrested by German forces Mar. 10, 1918,
                    on the Åland Islands when <hi rendition="#italic">en route</hi> from Petrograd
                    to Stockholm.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d121-f4" n="1" xml:id="d121-f4-content">
                <p>Lieut. Col. Raymond Robins, in charge of the American Red Cross Commission to
                    Russia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d121-f5" n="2" xml:id="d121-f5-content">
                <p>Capt. William B. Webster, member of the American Red Cross Commission to
                    Russia.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
